cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "dev-conventions: evidence-graded conventions for AI-assisted software development"
message: "If you cite this collection, please cite the version you read. Claims are corrected between versions and a corrected claim is otherwise indistinguishable from one that still holds."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Fejes"
    given-names: "Natanael"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-8674-0411"
version: 0.15.0
date-released: "2026-09-09"
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/natanaelfejes/dev-conventions"
abstract: >-
  A collection of conventions for AI-assisted software development in which every
  claim carries the strength of the evidence behind it, graded on an eight-tier
  scale ordered by review trail rather than by whether numbers exist. Guidance is
  conditional on a per-repository profile rather than prescribed. Disagreements
  between sources are left standing with a stated default. The collection keeps a
  numbered list of its own errors, which as of this version runs to twenty-nine
  and includes a fabricated statistic it carried for a day before a verification
  pass traced it to a source that does not exist, two figures published under a
  claim that the paper they came from had been read in full when it had not, a
  platform limit it invented, asserted without a source, and enforced in its own
  build for a week, and five days during which every correction it made reached
  the repository and none of them reached the copy that was actually loading.
keywords:
  - software engineering
  - coding agents
  - evidence grading
  - developer conventions
  - reproducibility
